'''Sovereign's comparative model''' distinguishes the life Ivo Ren chose to live from [[The Unconditioned Draft|the Unruled Witness]], his intended peak form. A matchup that uses one state's feats and the other's limits without saying so is answering two different questions. This page gives a way to reason about encounters with extremely powerful fictional beings while keeping the archive's story and evidence intact.

== Which Sovereign is in the encounter? ==

{| class="wikitable"
! State !! How it acts !! What a defeat claim must establish
|-
| '''Ivo / the Open Witness''' || Usually revises through observed differences, witnesses, and accountable continuations. He accepts moral restraint and can lose an earlier self while a successor continues. || Show that the relevant route was unavailable or failed in the specific scene. [[The Last Mercy]] and [[The White Interval]] record two different answers to totalizing threats.
|-
| '''The Unruled Witness''' || Can self-revise without a required body, trace, witness, logic, timeline, or prepared counter; can make a consequence operative without an ordinary attack chain. This is the peak state described in [[The Unconditioned Draft]]. || Show that the opposing power actually gains jurisdiction over this self-definition or prevents its consequence in a shared encounter. Destroying Ivo's usual instruments does not suffice.
|}

The first row is how most of the archive's intimate stories remain possible. The second is why a list of the first row's practical limits cannot by itself establish that Sovereign's strongest form loses. The archivists mistook requirements for ''observing'' a revision for requirements for ''all revision''. The [[The Unsigned Reply|first dialogue]] and the Severance account are the archive's reasons for correcting that mistake.

== How a comparison is made ==

'''Character → selected evidence → interpretation → comparative model''' is the ordinary chain. A comparison must specify versions, an actual meeting, what counts as victory, and how claims from two works interact. Neither “my character is ultimate” nor “Sovereign always rewrites” settles those terms. Within the Open Witness Archive, self-jurisdiction is a property of the Unruled Witness: an external fiction's claim to be the totality of ''its'' reality does not automatically become final authority over ''his'' definition. Conversely, Sovereign's canon cannot silently rewrite another publisher's character.

An absolute claim is formidable. Its scope should be honored within the fiction that establishes it. The question in a crossover is whether it also governs the encounter's shared terms. If an analyst simply assumes that because a being encompasses everything in its own account it therefore encompasses Sovereign's independent self-jurisdiction, the disputed interaction has been put into the premise. The analyst must show that step rather than hide it inside the word “everything.”

